Biopsy or Excision of Skin Lesion / Cyst
General Practice Other
Changing/symptomatic skin lesion, Bleeding/ulceration/crusting, Firm mobile subcutaneous nodule, Histopathology confirming diagnosis

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-003
Reason for Visit: Acute rupture and painful inflammation of a previously stable sebaceous cyst, causing severe localized distress and ongoing bleeding.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, spontaneous rupture of a large skin lesion accompanied by severe localized tenderness, recurrent bleeding, and purulent discharge. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to evaluate for spreading cellulitis, provide immediate hemostasis, manage acute pain, and formulate an immediate surgical excision or biopsy plan. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period risked uncontrolled infection and ongoing hemorrhage overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "The lump on my back suddenly burst, it's bleeding everywhere, and the pain is keeping me awake."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the presence of a clinically concerning, symptomatic skin lesion that underwent a rapid recent growth observed by the patient over the past 48 hours. Three hours ago, the lesion spontaneously ruptured, leading to a history of recurrent inflammation or purulent discharge from the cyst alongside recurrent bleeding, ulceration, or crusting of the non-melanocytic lesion. The patient notes severe localized tenderness upon palpation of the lesion and physical irritation due to friction from clothing. The rapid evolution of symptoms has caused significant distress and an inability to lie supine.
Functional Status: Independent, but mobility is currently restricted by severe back pain from the inflamed lesion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives alone and is unable to reach or dress the lesion independently on their posterior torso.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting positions frequently to avoid pressure on the back, no systemic toxicity.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the upper back reveals a changing, symptomatic skin lesion with bleeding, ulceration, and crusting. Palpation demonstrates a firm, mobile subcutaneous nodule with a central punctum in the case of a sebaceous cyst, surrounded by a 3cm rim of profound erythema and extreme localized tenderness. There is active, slow serosanguinous and purulent discharge. Expected investigation results include histopathological examination of the excised tissue confirming the exact cellular nature and determining margin clearance of the lesion.
Environmental Observations: Blood-stained bedsheets; home environment is clean and well-lit.
Medication Reconciliation: No current antibiotics or anticoagulants; verified verbally.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Biopsy or Excision of Skin Lesion / Cyst (Acutely Inflamed Sebaceous Cyst) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Malignant Melanoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of a central punctum, purulent discharge, and a firm mobile subcutaneous structure rather than a superficial pigmented, asymmetric lesion.

Cutaneous Abscess requiring immediate ED drainage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of systemic fever, lack of fluctuance extending beyond the discrete nodule, and stable vital signs.

Lipoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of active bleeding, purulent discharge, and a visible central punctum which are uncharacteristic of a simple lipoma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced an acute rupture and infection of a sebaceous cyst, resulting in persistent bleeding, purulent discharge, and severe localized pain preventing sleep. Telephone triage accurately identified the potential for an expanding cutaneous abscess or uncontrolled bleeding. Face-to-face assessment was urgently required to clean the wound, apply a pressure dressing for hemostasis, rule out spreading cellulitis requiring intravenous antibiotics, and document informed consent for subsequent surgical excision and local anaesthesia. Delaying until the next in-hours period would have resulted in untreated local infection, intractable pain, and high likelihood of ED presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Thorough wound irrigation and application of a heavy sterile pressure dressing. Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg QID for 5 days. Documented informed consent for formal surgical excision to be scheduled in-hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote healing and prevent secondary infection, commit to leaving the dressing entirely undisturbed for 48 hours. Reinforce this by placing an alarm on your phone for two days from now to serve as the exclusive time to check the wound. Removing the constant urge to touch the lesion reduces pain and limits bacterial introduction, positively reinforcing hands-off wound care.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sterile dressings and tape (provided from bag).
Follow-up: GP review within 48 hours for wound check and scheduling of definitive excision.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek immediate emergency medical care via 000 or the nearest Emergency Department if you develop a high fever, rapidly spreading redness beyond the dressing, worsening severe pain, or confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute inflammation and pain are expected to decrease significantly within 48 hours of commencing oral antibiotics. Definitive resolution requires complete surgical excision of the cyst capsule.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Return for urgent evaluation if the bleeding soaks entirely through the heavy pressure dressing tonight, or if you develop a systemic fever or rigors.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Antibiotics are needed to treat the surrounding skin infection, while the pressure dressing will stop the bleeding. The cyst will inevitably refill and rupture again unless the entire sac is surgically removed once the acute infection settles.
Actionable Steps: Take your antibiotics exactly as prescribed. Do not attempt to squeeze or further drain the cyst yourself. Sleep on your side tonight.

